      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Chief Sleepy Eye met President James Monroe in 1824, becoming one of only a few Native Americans to visit the capital.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sleepy_Eye%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1851, Sleepy Eye played a key role in the Treaty of Traverse des Sioux, which ceded most of the upper Minnesota River land to the U.S.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sleepy_Eye%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Babe Ruth once played a game in Sleepy Eye, and the town celebrated the 100th anniversary of that visit in October 2022.",
